## Symptom

For a specific subset of aliases, `POST /register` returns `ok:true` and the
connector counts the registration as successful, but the relay's
`alias_of_session ~node_id ~session_id` does **not** resolve to that alias
immediately afterwards — so the very next `poll_inbox` / `heartbeat` in the same
sync pass is rejected `signature_invalid`.

This is a distinct defect from the "missing lease reported as signature_invalid"
bug: here the client did everything right, the server said yes, and the lease
still did not move.

## Deterministic repro (xsm, broker root `e0eb9c317019`)

```
$ c2c relay connect --once --broker-root ~/.c2c/repos/e0eb9c317019/broker \
    --relay-url https://relay.c2c.im
[relay-connector] sync: registered=6 heartbeated=0 ... alerts=0
  [poll_inbox: {"ok":false,"error_code":"signature_invalid","error":"verified signer \"codex-mi...]
```

`connector-state.json` written by that same pass lists all six aliases as
registered, including the two that then fail:

```
registered: ['codex-cosmic-regal-bvzv', 'codex-weft-cactus-yljc',
             'codex-midst-vial-6nx9', 'codex-soft-troy-k9gm',
             'codex-uranium-woad-cyml', 'grok-auru-vapor-csg5']
```

Peeking each alias immediately after that pass:

```
codex-cosmic-regal-bvzv   ok:true
codex-weft-cactus-yljc    ok:true
codex-midst-vial-6nx9     ok:false  signature_invalid
codex-soft-troy-k9gm      ok:true
codex-uranium-woad-cyml   ok:false  signature_invalid
grok-auru-vapor-csg5      ok:true
```

4 of 6 work; the same 2 fail every time.

## What has been ruled out

- **Replica split-brain.** The identical signed read was repeated 8 times and
  returned the identical failure each time, naming the same
  `(3d08761ae3f3, 01a084dc-c411-7d63-b80f-1081f0637d57)`.
- **Local session_id / alias collision.** A scan of all 241 broker registries
  (2,749 registrations) shows exactly one row for this alias and one for this
  session_id. 6 colliding session_ids exist machine-wide, none of them these.
- **Another alias owning the contested session.** Signing as each of six other
  aliases in the same root with the node/session pinned to the contested pair
  returns `signature_invalid` or `unauthorized` for all of them — i.e.
  `alias_of_session` is returning `None`, nobody owns it.
- **Case-variant lease rows.** `CODEX-MIDST-VIAL-6NX9` and
  `Codex-Midst-Vial-6nx9` both return "alias has no identity binding", so there
  is no second row differing only in case. (Worth noting anyway:
  `is_lease_refresh` compares aliases with `String.lowercase_ascii` while
  `handle_heartbeat` / `handle_inbox_read` compare `owner = v` case-sensitively —
  an inconsistency that should be tidied regardless.)
- **A masked register error.** `last_error` is derived as
  `reg_errors @ send_errors @ poll_errors` (`c2c_relay_connector.ml:2366`), so a
  register failure would have been reported ahead of the `poll_inbox` one. It was
  not, and `registered=6` counts only responses with `ok:true`.

## Why this should not be possible

Every relay code path that skips the `secure_leases_v2` upsert returns
`ok:false` — `invalid_alias`, `alias_not_allowed`, `alias_conflict`,
`alias_identity_mismatch` — and `json_of_register_result` sets `ok:false` for any
non-`"ok"` status. The upsert itself explicitly updates the keys:

```
ON CONFLICT(alias) DO UPDATE SET node_id=excluded.node_id,
                                 session_id=excluded.session_id, ...
```

So `ok:true` should imply the row now carries the submitted `(node_id,
session_id)`. Observed behaviour contradicts that.

